      "title": "Vision, prescriptions and specified disease",
      "narration": "Vision benefits usually cover an exam and an allowance toward lenses or frames on a schedule. Prescription drug benefits may be built into a medical plan or sold separately, with copayments that differ by drug tier. Specified disease, or dread disease, policies pay only if a named illness such as cancer is diagnosed, often as a lump sum or scheduled amount regardless of other coverage. They are supplements, not substitutes for medical insurance. A producer who presents a cancer policy as full health coverage misleads the buyer and may violate Maryland’s advertising rules.",
